<h2 id="deploying-the-application" class="anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-tighter"><span>Deploying the application</span><a class="rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden" href="#deploying-the-application" aria-label="Link to Deploying the application"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 14 14" class="inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3.5"><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="1.4" d="m3.938 12.688 1.75-11.375M8.313 12.688l1.75-11.375M3.063 4.813h8.75M2.188 9.188h8.75"></path></svg></a></h2>
<p>When you’re ready to deploy your React application, you can use any static site hosting service such as Vercel or Netlify. Ensure that your environment variables are correctly configured in the deployment settings.</p>
<p>Since this example relies on client‑side routing with React Router, you’ll also need to define rewrite rules.</p>
<p>For example, if you’re deploying to Vercel, add a <code>vercel.json</code> file to the root of your project with the following content:</p>
